# Let the Bluetooth radio wake the machine from suspend. Installed to
# /etc/udev/rules.d/ by `make install-udev` — system config, so it is not
# stowed.
#
# Why this exists: with the lid shut, pressing a key on the Bluetooth keyboard
# would not wake the laptop, and after waking it some other way the keyboard
# stayed dead for roughly 45 seconds. Those are two symptoms of one cause.
#
# The kernel leaves power/wakeup on the Intel controller (8087:0026) set to
# "disabled" by default, even though the device advertises Remote Wakeup in its
# USB descriptor (bmAttributes 0xe0) and BlueZ already has the keyboard marked
# WakeAllowed=yes. With wakeup disabled the controller is not armed, so btusb
# lets it go down hard across s2idle and the link state is lost:
#
#   bluetoothd: Controller resume with wake event 0x0     <- BT did not wake us
#   hci0: ACL packet for unknown connection handle 512    <- keyboard still
#                                                            talking over a link
#                                                            the host forgot
#   connect to DC:2C:26:F3:51:F3: Operation already in progress (114)
#
# The keyboard believes the pre-suspend link is still up and keeps sending on
# the old handle; the controller comes back with no such handle and drops the
# packets, while BlueZ's reconnect attempts fail against the stale connection
# object still held in the kernel. Nothing recovers until the link supervision
# timeout finally expires — hence the wait.
#
# Arming the controller as a wake source keeps the link alive across suspend,
# which addresses both halves at once.
#
# The alternative that comes up first when searching — a systemd-sleep hook
# that disconnects HID devices before suspending — makes reconnect fast but
# permanently rules out waking by keyboard, because no link is left to wake
# over. It trades the goal for the symptom. Do not "fix" this that way.
#
#   make verify-udev     show whether the radio is currently armed
#
# To undo for a single boot, without touching this rule:
#   echo disabled | sudo tee /sys/bus/usb/devices/3-14/power/wakeup

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="8087", ATTR{idProduct}=="0026", ATTR{power/wakeup}="enabled"
